<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Carantania</h1><p class="st-meta">658 CE – 828 CE (approx.) · capital Karnburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An early Slavic principality in the eastern Alps, ancestor of Carinthia, famous for its unusual ceremony of installing dukes on the Prince's Stone.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Alpine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in the late 6th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> overlordship. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samo's</a> death around 658 they formed an independent principality centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollfeld">Zollfeld</a> plain near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnburg">Karnburg</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>. A ruler named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valuk">Valuk</a> appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Fredegar</a> as 'duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a>' around 631.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Around 743 Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borut">Borut</a> sought Bavarian help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and accepted Bavarian overlordship; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorazd-of-carantania">Gorazd</a> and nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hotimir">Hotimir</a> were raised as Christians, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> led the mission. Princes were installed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princes-stone">Prince's Stone</a> (Knežji kamen) in a ceremony led by a peasant speaking Slavic, a rite later used for dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a pagan revolt in the 770s, Carantania passed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> under Frankish rule in 788. Following the uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljudevit-posavski">Ljudevit Posavski</a>, the native princes were replaced by Frankish counts in 828. The land became the core of the later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-carinthia">Duchy of Carinthia</a> and is a landmark in Slovene history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/carantania.jpg" alt="Carantania" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: Kmiki87; derivative work by Bostjan46 Waterways_of_SE... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carantania_800_AD-en.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>658 CE – 828 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Karnburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Borut, Gorazd, Hotimir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alpine Slavic (early Slovene)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karantanija, Principality of Carantania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=743&amp;focus=carantania" aria-label="Carantania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5017 537 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Carantania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5337 793L5279 723M5337 793L5139 672M5337 793L5336 795"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5279 723h0M5139 672h0M5336 795h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5337 793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5337 793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=743&amp;focus=carantania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
